16	// verdict by name, and the literal code. The codes stay written literal
17	// (0/2/12/15) rather than imported from the module on purpose: importing the
18	// constants would let a renumbering pass green.
19	//
20	// Two ways of running it, because the code half and the process half are now
21	// separable. `resolve` calls the resolver in-process, prints its returned
22	// verdict through the very wrapper the CLI prints through, and reports the code
23	// that wrapper hands to `process.exit` — so the exit code stays half the
24	// contract, asserted on the value that becomes it. `run` spawns the REAL CLI,
25	// and the cases below that still use it are the ones whose subject IS the
26	// process: the exit-code rule over every verdict, the usage exit, and the
27	// vendored copy resolving against its own root from another cwd. Those three
28	// keep a real `process.exit` status under assertion end to end; the rest judge
29	// the decision, which is what they were always reading.
